{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat > paper_strategy.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\",\n  \\\"selected_prompt_family\\\": \\\"paper_standalone\\\",\n  \\\"recommended_title\\\": \\\"Local Residual Scaling for Compositional Conformal Prediction: Benefits and Limits\\\",\n  \\\"central_question\\\": \\\"When does training-only local residual scaling improve the coverage–reference-mass tradeoff of split-conformal regions for continuous compositions, and what do homoscedastic and covariate-shift controls reveal about its limits?\\\",\n  \\\"central_thesis\\\": \\\"In the evaluated logistic-normal settings, the frozen training-only scale-selection procedure improves measured coverage–reference-mass tradeoffs over global ILR calibration under boundary-concentrated heteroscedasticity, provides no consistent homoscedastic advantage, and performs favorably under the tested shift without establishing shifted-distribution validity or a contribution attributable specifically to quantile stumps.\\\",\n  \\\"narrative_rationale\\\": \\\"This is a controlled empirical study of a positive scale function inside a fixed compositional conformal construction, not a new geometry, universal reliability guarantee, or search-success narrative. Six final seed episodes provide matched global-ILR, raw-simplex and Bonferroni comparisons, two nominal levels, fixed subgroups, reference-mass estimates, controls, and paired aggregate uncertainty. The strong contrast between homoscedastic and boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic conditions supplies a coherent scientific question; shifted cases supply a limited stress test rather than another validity theorem. Direct source inspection identifies a complete ridge/constant/tail-blend selection procedure, but no selected-component diagnostics or matched final ridge-only comparison. Conditional empirical benefits therefore beat both a universal-superiority story and an unsupported stump-specific mechanism story. A latent-count bridge was not implemented and an interaction extension has no usable performance evidence. Publication-worthiness is a framing judgment, not an additional qualification result.\\\",\n  \\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\": \\\"recentered\\\",\n  \\\"edit_framing_decision\\\": \\\"Edit mode is not evidenced: no resolved edit-context file, previous_paper directory, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is present. No prior manuscript is claimed to have been revised. The enum 'recentered' describes centering the broad source-task/proposal context on the actually evaluated local-scale procedure. Compared candidate framings were: preserve broad compositional reliability or latent-target motivation; broaden to a shared count-noise/interaction/scale family; recenter on matched residual-scale evidence. The first two exceed the implemented and evaluated scope; the third is selected.\\\",\n  \\\"staged_input_impact\\\": [\n    \\\"No prior manuscript or separate staged new/old implementation package was supplied; edit placeholders remain unresolved. Task and evaluated implementation files, rather than a prior title or figure plan, determine the story.\\\",\n    \\\"The final_evaluation object was read independently of downstream_study, which is absent. The declared qualification sources report completed, qualified, and true; this run has no failed final qualification to disclose. Candidate self-diagnostics are explicitly unverified, and unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown.\\\",\n    \\\"Direct inspection of results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_tail_adaptive_residual_scaling/program_1/src/program.py and METHOD.md establishes the full training-only scale-selection family. The final result manifest and program-tree hash identify the frozen evaluated selection; no exact-byte hash match between the workspace source and frozen tree was independently recomputed, so source-to-final linkage is documented rather than newly cryptographically verified.\\\",\n    \\\"task/evaluate_final.py and task/resources/final/manifest.json establish H/B/S logistic-normal conditions in dimensions 3 and 10, distinct center/scale/calibration/test splits, shared centers, fixed groups and reference probes. These change the research scope from generic classifier probabilities to continuous simplex-valued responses.\\\",\n    \\\"The original quadratic-feature ridge scale is available as code and search context, but not as a matched repeated-final comparator. Do not substitute the global-ILR baseline for it or compare its search score to final scores. No study.* sources exist here, so do not report selection deltas or scored/unscored attempt counts as study-grounded findings.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"method_groups\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Training-only local scale selection in ILR space\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"proposed\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The frozen evaluated procedure selects among a quadratic-feature ridge log-scale, a constant, and four ridge/tail log-space blends using three training folds, fitting-only alignment, and a one-standard-error heuristic. Describe and evaluate the entire procedure, not a proven quantile-stump winner.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Global ILR split conformal\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Primary matched comparator: same training-only ridge center, ILR Euclidean residual geometry, calibration split and corrected rank, without covariate-dependent scaling. It isolates scale adaptivity more cleanly than cross-geometry comparisons and is not the original ridge-scale implementation.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Raw-simplex L-infinity split conformal\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Grounded alternative representation and region shape, evaluated on the same target compositions, splits, centers and reference probes. It contextualizes the ILR comparison without claiming geometry is independently ablated.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Coordinatewise Bonferroni regions\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Grounded simultaneous-coordinate comparator using corrected per-coordinate calibration at alpha divided by dimension; expose its coverage and reference-mass tradeoffs rather than reducing comparison to one aggregate score.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Constant-scale and permuted-residual controls\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"supporting\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Training controls examine scale invariance and the dependence on covariate–residual alignment. Constant scale numerically reproduces global ILR; do not plot it as another independent baseline. Permuted residuals support an alignment interpretation but do not isolate stumps, blend weights, or the selection rule.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Calibration and degenerate-region sanity comparators\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"supporting\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Uncalibrated residual quantiles, the full simplex and a singleton center are available for supplementary checks of coverage–mass interpretation. They are not competing learned methods and need not dominate the main narrative.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"results_focus\\\": [\n    \\\"Main comparison: four scientifically meaningful methods, grouped by homoscedastic (H), boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic (B), and shifted heteroscedastic (S) conditions, with dimension 3 versus 10 treated as adaptations under one thesis rather than independent experiments.\\\",\n    \\\"Show coverage at nominal 0.95 and 0.90 separately, not only absolute errors. Show reference mass separately from coverage so that an apparently small region cannot hide undercoverage. Efficiency is probability mass under the fixed mixed probe distribution, not geometric volume, expected cardinality, or a distribution-free quantity.\\\",\n    \\\"Keep fixed-subgroup evidence in the main paper: a compact six-condition table compares the local procedure, global ILR and permuted-residual control using mean absolute subgroup coverage error over both groups and both levels. Supporting tables retain the signed per-group, per-level coverage and per-seed Wilson intervals; empirical group diagnostics are not conditional guarantees.\\\",\n    \\\"Homoscedastic cases show little benefit and small aggregate-utility disadvantages relative to global ILR; do not omit them. Boundary heteroscedastic cases support the strongest joint subgroup-error/reference-mass comparison. Boundary concentration and heteroscedasticity change together, so neither factor is independently causally identified.\\\",\n    \\\"Shifted cases have favorable observed coverage relative to the matched global comparator, but exchangeability is not supplied by the unweighted algorithm. In shifted dimension 3 the local method has larger reference mass than global ILR, so do not claim uniformly smaller regions or a free efficiency gain.\\\",\n    \\\"An aggregate main-text table may report mean utility 0.8772268474776957 with its stored 95% seed-bootstrap interval [0.8766236679621646, 0.8781275287778212], mean marginal absolute coverage error 0.011729600694444453, mean fixed-group absolute coverage error 0.015105333854689552, and reference mass 0.4508402082655165, with sensible rounding. Sources: final.metric.* entries with matching names. Define utility from evaluator code as 1 - [0.375 marginal error + 0.375 group error + 0.25 reference mass]; it is a chosen evaluation weighting, not universal reliability.\\\",\n    \\\"The aggregate local-minus-global utility difference is 0.04762325792650014 (final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap); its stored paired six-seed bootstrap interval is [0.043901240346666696, 0.050616967867983816] (final.evidence.episodes). Interpret it as a fixed-six-case aggregate, not evidence of per-case superiority or proof that stumps improve the original ridge-scale method.\\\",\n    \\\"Final uncertainty uses six seeds [1009, 2003, 3001, 4001, 5003, 6007] and stored 2000-resample paired seed-bootstrap intervals. Repeated copies of the same aggregate interval inside episodes are one estimate, not six independent estimates. Coverage Wilson intervals describe within-seed test hits; do not average interval endpoints or use a mass Wilson interval as an independent-query population interval.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"figure_plan\\\": [\n    \\\"Figure 1, early conceptual overview: interior-simplex response -> ILR center and training-only positive scale -> held-out normalized-score calibration -> separate coverage, subgroup and reference-mass evaluation; mark the shifted condition outside the exchangeability guarantee.\\\",\n    \\\"Figure 2, main reliability comparison: four panels in a 2-by-2 grid for dimension and nominal level, comparing the four main methods across H/B/S with all six seed points and an arithmetic-mean marker. Nominal coverage is a horizontal line, not an attainment-rate label.\\\",\n    \\\"Figure 3, companion efficiency comparison: two dimension panels comparing reference mass across H/B/S and the same four methods, explicitly averaged over the two nominal levels within each seed. Read alongside Figure 2 and the main subgroup table, never as a standalone superiority ranking.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"figure_specs\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"conceptual_overview\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Make the target, training/calibration separation, learned scale, and limited meaning of the evidence legible before quantitative results.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"diagram\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"generate-concept-figure\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"scientific_object_and_information_split\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"not_applicable_conceptual_only\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"training_calibration_and_evaluation_roles\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"The inspected ICLR paper.sty gives one 5.5-inch text column and a 9-inch text height; full width is also column width here. Target a 5.5-by-1.9-inch overview on page 1 or 2, about 0.25 page including caption. One left-to-right schematic with at most six short labeled blocks and a shift caveat is readable at 9-point text; avoid mini-panels, data-shaped decorative regions and invented observations.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 1,\n      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 1,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"coverage_by_condition\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Expose condition-dependent empirical reliability, both nominal levels, and the absence of a uniform advantage.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"faceted_seed_dot_plot\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"H_B_S_condition\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"empirical_joint_composition_coverage\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"dimension_3_or_10_and_nominal_coverage_0.95_or_0.90\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Use the full 5.5-inch single-column width, approximately 4.2 inches high, about 0.55 page including caption. Four panels in two rows leave each plotting area roughly 2.5 inches wide; restrict to three condition labels, four method colors, six small offset seed dots per method and one shared legend. Labels at least 9 points, ticks at least 8 points; use the same coverage axis across panels and avoid connecting H/B/S as a trajectory. Show seed variability rather than inventing condition-specific bootstrap intervals.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 4,\n      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"reference_mass_by_condition\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Compare the specific measured size proxy without hiding coverage failures or the shifted dimension-3 mass increase.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"faceted_seed_dot_plot\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"H_B_S_condition\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"reference_mass_averaged_over_nominal_levels_within_seed\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"dimension_3_or_10\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Target 5.5 by 2.5 inches across two side-by-side panels, about 0.35 page including caption. Three categories and four shared method series fit without shrinking labels; use seed dots, mean markers, a shared legend and a common mass axis. State the alpha averaging and mixed reference measure explicitly. Do not combine with the four-panel coverage figure into an unreadable six-panel figure.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"supplementary_plan\\\": {\n    \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Provide reconstructable methods, complete observed uncertainty and diagnostics, and transparency about missing comparisons after references, while leaving every result and limitation essential to the central thesis in the main paper.\\\",\n    \\\"planned_subsections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Complete Conditional Scale-Selection Specification\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Use the inspected selected program and METHOD.md for the quadratic ridge features, tail target 0.9, 15 candidate threshold quantiles, minimum leaf size, learning rate 0.1, maximum 80 stump updates, three deterministic folds, constant and four log blends, fitting-only alignment, simplicity ordering, all-data refit, scale floors and evaluator clipping. Explain that the one-standard-error rule is a heuristic, not a significance test. Include pseudocode derived from source, not selected-component frequencies that were never recorded. Final evidence binds the evaluated method; source linkage is documentary unless independently verified later.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Generator, Splits, Geometry and Reference Measure\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document task/evaluate_final.py and task/resources/final/manifest.json: six-dimensional Gaussian covariates; H/B/S generator equations; dimension 3/10; center-fit, scale-fit, calibration and test sizes 1024/1024/512/2048; the test first-covariate shift 1.25; shared ridge center; exact ILR basis; strictly positive responses; 128 selected test covariates and 1024 probes per query from a half Gaussian-in-ILR/half uniform-simplex mixture. Explain query clustering, data-dependent reference covariance, and why mass is not geometric volume. No exact-zero, finite-count or real-data experiment is implied.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Full Seed-Level Coverage and Efficiency Tables\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Tabulate all final cases and seeds, both alphas, both fixed sign-of-first-covariate groups, all four main methods, and available within-seed Wilson intervals. Retain signed coverage deviations as well as absolute errors and reference mass; do not infer general conditional coverage. Show stored aggregate paired intervals once and describe the fixed-case, seed-resampling unit. Tables can be generated solely from the final.evidence.episodes pointer.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.marginal_coverage_error\\\", \\\"final.metric.group_coverage_error\\\", \\\"final.metric.reference_mass\\\", \\\"final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Alignment, Calibration and Degenerate-Region Checks\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Report constant/global numerical agreement, permuted-training-residual results, uncalibrated residual-quantile baseline, full-simplex and singleton-center coverage/mass, using their stored final records. State what each check tests and cannot establish. Do not invent a stump-only, ridge-only, no-selection, interaction-depth, sample-size, dimension-sweep or weighted-shift ablation.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.constant_control_score\\\", \\\"final.metric.permuted_control_score\\\", \\\"final.metric.control_pass_fraction\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Conditional Rank Argument and Its Boundaries\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Give the standard split-conformal rank argument conditional on the center-fit and scale-fit data: a fixed positive scale preserves exchangeable calibration/test scores; k=ceil((m+1)(1-alpha)) with infinity for k>m gives marginal coverage under the stated assumptions. Cover ties and common clipping. Do not label it a new theorem, a group-coverage guarantee, or shifted-distribution validity; novelty and supporting citations need independent literature verification.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Reproducibility and Evaluation Scope\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document the available NumPy 2.1.3 requirement, evaluator Python 3.12 statement, six final seeds, synthetic-data generation and no external-data download, artifact hashes, available commands and per-seed stored elapsed durations with their limited meaning. Distinguish inspected code, recorded executions and commands verified later. Transparently record that the latent-count bridge was not implemented and the interaction attempt has no usable measured performance; root cause is unknown. Search counts and deltas are omitted because study.* sources are absent. Final qualification is completed/qualified, not proof of generalization. Provide anonymous relative reproduction paths and a table/figure-to-generator mapping when those scripts exist.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\", \\\"final.qualification.correct\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"LLM Usage\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Disclose the evidenced roles of LLM assistance in ideation, candidate-source editing, framing, literature-query planning and subsequent writing, without asserting unrecorded manual verification or completed experiments. Avoid model/version attributions unless logged. Record prohibited-literature encounters only if an actual encounter is documented; none was encountered through network access in this strategy stage.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \\\"forbidden_headings\\\": [\n    \\\"Search Progression\\\", \\\"Campaign Phases\\\", \\\"Milestones\\\", \\\"First Valid Result\\\", \\\"Iteration History\\\", \\\"Experiment A\\\", \\\"Experiment B\\\", \\\"Winner Selection\\\", \\\"From Failure to Success\\\", \\\"Latent-Count Bridge Results\\\", \\\"Quantile-Stump Ablation\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"forbidden_phrases\\\": [\n    \\\"we then tried\\\", \\\"subsequent improvements\\\", \\\"first valid result\\\", \\\"next-best\\\", \\\"later variants\\\", \\\"search winner\\\", \\\"correctness rate\\\", \\\"correct and eligible\\\", \\\"eligible\\\", \\\"combined_score\\\", \\\"rubric\\\", \\\"worker_01\\\", \\\"worker_02\\\", \\\"state of the art\\\", \\\"universally reliable\\\", \\\"uniformly smaller regions\\\", \\\"guaranteed coverage under covariate shift\\\", \\\"distribution-free conditional validity\\\", \\\"quantile stumps caused the gain\\\", \\\"the latent bridge restored coverage\\\", \\\"unscored attempts were measured losses\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"notes_for_writer\\\": [\n    \\\"Draft no paper during strategy generation. This plan is for write-paper-standalone. Later writing, review, audit and revision must independently re-read final.qualification.*; retain any failed qualification and its limitations if the authoritative artifacts change. Here completed/qualified/true is explicit; unspecified diagnostics remain unknown, and no statistical-versus-adaptivity cause should be invented.\\\",\n    \\\"Qualification source IDs are final.qualification.status, final.qualification.outcome and final.qualification.correct. Scientific result records are final.evidence.episodes and final.metric.*. The episode grounding entry is a pointer, not an embedded value; follow its json_pointer to the actual episodes. Source-inspected equations and implementation facts additionally require the named local files; do not imply those details were candidate diagnostics verified by the evaluator.\\\",\n    \\\"Use ICLR 2026 per VENUE.md, not a newly chosen conference. Inspected .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex and paper.sty: a single 5.5-inch column, 9-inch text height, normal float width equal to text width. All required figures use preferred_span=full_width meaning that sole text column; no fictitious two-column 0.48-width placement. Keep body between 8 and 9 pages; target 8.5 substantive pages and at least 75% occupancy if ending at 8 pages.\\\",\n    \\\"Body allocation including associated floats: abstract/introduction with early overview 1.25 pages; focused related work 0.65; target, geometry, scale procedure and short rank justification 1.85; experimental design, metrics, controls and uncertainty 1.15; condition-structured results with coverage/mass figures and two concise tables 2.40; discussion and limitations 0.95; conclusion 0.25. Total 8.50 pages. Use extra space for the exact reference-measure definition, subgroup contrasts and mechanism caveats, never chronology, repeated tables or generic filler. Keep the main-text limitations and negative H results even if page pressure requires moving detailed pseudocode after references.\\\",\n    \\\"Suggested main headings: Introduction; Related Work; Compositional Regions and Training-Only Scale Learning; Evaluation Design; Coverage and Efficiency Across Conditions; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Within results use Homoscedastic Controls, Boundary-Concentrated Heteroscedasticity, and Covariate-Shift Stress Test. These are three conditions of one study, not three discovery stages.\\\",\n    \\\"Main Table 1: four-method aggregate utility and decomposed errors/mass, with stored local-minus-comparator paired intervals explicitly aggregate over the fixed six cases. Main Table 2: six-condition local/global/permuted subgroup-error comparison. Do not manufacture cell-specific confidence intervals from the overall paired interval; distinguish descriptive means from stored inferential summaries.\\\",\n    \\\"Required quantitative figures must derive every seed point from final.evidence.episodes, export PDF and PNG from the same source with PNG at 300 DPI, and use the PDF in LaTeX whenever available. All full-width raster-only assets must be regenerated at at least 2400 pixels wide, never upscaled; any later column-width-only raster must be at least 1200 pixels wide. At most one overview uses generate-concept-figure; do not create that image in this strategy stage. Preserve editable concept source and provenance later, and record any necessary generator fallback rather than silently changing ownership.\\\",\n    \\\"The shared reference distribution is a training-dependent mixture, not invariant simplex volume. Probe-hit Wilson intervals are evaluator-provided binomial-style summaries over clustered query/probe hits; they do not quantify all query-sampling uncertainty. Six seeds and a fixed condition grid limit bootstrap precision and external validity. Do not pool all test examples across seeds as if one independently fitted model generated them.\\\",\n    \\\"Do not equate composition-region coverage with class-label prediction-set coverage, calibration of class probabilities, latent multinomial-probability recovery, or true conditional-probability identification. Outputs here are strictly positive continuous logistic-normal responses, not softmax predictions from an evaluated classifier, exact-zero compositions, CIFAR data or finite-count observations.\\\",\n    \\\"No recorded component-selection frequency, matched repeated ridge-scale comparison, interaction ablation, sensitivity study or real-data test exists. Neither control success nor a favorable aggregate utility attributes the effect to quantile stumps. The original ridge scale remains a documented component and an untested matched-final comparator, not a demonstrated inferior method.\\\",\n    \\\"References and provenance exist, but retrieval metadata alone does not establish relevance or that full text was read. Lei2016DistributionFreePI has a directly relevant title match and is in the allowed starter lineage; verify actual consulted content before citation. Do not fill Related Work with unrelated automatically retrieved entries. Verify suitable ILR, quantile and conditional-coverage context through permitted sources before claiming novelty. Do not identify, search for, open or use the prohibited related paper or citing/discussing pages; stop and record any accidental encounter. This strategy made no network requests.\\\",\n    \\\"For double-blind review remove author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding and identifying links; do not copy the template's explicit author or final-copy setting. Preserve any required automatically managed watermark block. Include separate LLM Usage after references and any actually required venue checklist; do not invent a checklist requirement from another conference or claim this local style was independently certified as the latest official release.\\\",\n    \\\"All supplementary source_ids below are registered result anchors; implementation, resource and process statements also name their actual local documentary sources and are not falsely recast as measured findings. For LLM-use details use the available process logs and this strategy's record, not the episode pointer as sole evidence. No definitive posthoc analysis ranking is assigned; route the separately authoritative appendix-ranked analyses to Supplementary Information without revising this plan retroactively.\\\",\n    \\\"Keep qualitative memory private. Do not use global_memory_summary.md, idea names, or old incomplete status prose to override completed final results. Internal names inside this strategy are private-only grounding instructions, never recommended visible manuscript labels. No final-paper validation, paper.tex, or claims_grounding.json is produced in this stage.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n}\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat > paper_strategy.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\",\n  \\\"selected_prompt_family\\\": \\\"paper_standalone\\\",\n  \\\"recommended_title\\\": \\\"Local Residual Scaling for Compositional Conformal Prediction: Benefits and Limits\\\",\n  \\\"central_question\\\": \\\"When does training-only local residual scaling improve the coverage–reference-mass tradeoff of split-conformal regions for continuous compositions, and what do homoscedastic and covariate-shift controls reveal about its limits?\\\",\n  \\\"central_thesis\\\": \\\"In the evaluated logistic-normal settings, the frozen training-only scale-selection procedure improves measured coverage–reference-mass tradeoffs over global ILR calibration under boundary-concentrated heteroscedasticity, provides no consistent homoscedastic advantage, and performs favorably under the tested shift without establishing shifted-distribution validity or a contribution attributable specifically to quantile stumps.\\\",\n  \\\"narrative_rationale\\\": \\\"This is a controlled empirical study of a positive scale function inside a fixed compositional conformal construction, not a new geometry, universal reliability guarantee, or search-success narrative. Six final seed episodes provide matched global-ILR, raw-simplex and Bonferroni comparisons, two nominal levels, fixed subgroups, reference-mass estimates, controls, and paired aggregate uncertainty. The strong contrast between homoscedastic and boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic conditions supplies a coherent scientific question; shifted cases supply a limited stress test rather than another validity theorem. Direct source inspection identifies a complete ridge/constant/tail-blend selection procedure, but no selected-component diagnostics or matched final ridge-only comparison. Conditional empirical benefits therefore beat both a universal-superiority story and an unsupported stump-specific mechanism story. A latent-count bridge was not implemented and an interaction extension has no usable performance evidence. Publication-worthiness is a framing judgment, not an additional qualification result.\\\",\n  \\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\": \\\"recentered\\\",\n  \\\"edit_framing_decision\\\": \\\"Edit mode is not evidenced: no resolved edit-context file, previous_paper directory, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is present. No prior manuscript is claimed to have been revised. The enum 'recentered' describes centering the broad source-task/proposal context on the actually evaluated local-scale procedure. Compared candidate framings were: preserve broad compositional reliability or latent-target motivation; broaden to a shared count-noise/interaction/scale family; recenter on matched residual-scale evidence. The first two exceed the implemented and evaluated scope; the third is selected.\\\",\n  \\\"staged_input_impact\\\": [\n    \\\"No prior manuscript or separate staged new/old implementation package was supplied; edit placeholders remain unresolved. Task and evaluated implementation files, rather than a prior title or figure plan, determine the story.\\\",\n    \\\"The final_evaluation object was read independently of downstream_study, which is absent. The declared qualification sources report completed, qualified, and true; this run has no failed final qualification to disclose. Candidate self-diagnostics are explicitly unverified, and unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown.\\\",\n    \\\"Direct inspection of results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_tail_adaptive_residual_scaling/program_1/src/program.py and METHOD.md establishes the full training-only scale-selection family. The final result manifest and program-tree hash identify the frozen evaluated selection; no exact-byte hash match between the workspace source and frozen tree was independently recomputed, so source-to-final linkage is documented rather than newly cryptographically verified.\\\",\n    \\\"task/evaluate_final.py and task/resources/final/manifest.json establish H/B/S logistic-normal conditions in dimensions 3 and 10, distinct center/scale/calibration/test splits, shared centers, fixed groups and reference probes. These change the research scope from generic classifier probabilities to continuous simplex-valued responses.\\\",\n    \\\"The original quadratic-feature ridge scale is available as code and search context, but not as a matched repeated-final comparator. Do not substitute the global-ILR baseline for it or compare its search score to final scores. No study.* sources exist here, so do not report selection deltas or scored/unscored attempt counts as study-grounded findings.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"method_groups\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Training-only local scale selection in ILR space\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"proposed\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The frozen evaluated procedure selects among a quadratic-feature ridge log-scale, a constant, and four ridge/tail log-space blends using three training folds, fitting-only alignment, and a one-standard-error heuristic. Describe and evaluate the entire procedure, not a proven quantile-stump winner.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Global ILR split conformal\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Primary matched comparator: same training-only ridge center, ILR Euclidean residual geometry, calibration split and corrected rank, without covariate-dependent scaling. It isolates scale adaptivity more cleanly than cross-geometry comparisons and is not the original ridge-scale implementation.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Raw-simplex L-infinity split conformal\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Grounded alternative representation and region shape, evaluated on the same target compositions, splits, centers and reference probes. It contextualizes the ILR comparison without claiming geometry is independently ablated.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Coordinatewise Bonferroni regions\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Grounded simultaneous-coordinate comparator using corrected per-coordinate calibration at alpha divided by dimension; expose its coverage and reference-mass tradeoffs rather than reducing comparison to one aggregate score.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Constant-scale and permuted-residual controls\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"supporting\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Training controls examine scale invariance and the dependence on covariate–residual alignment. Constant scale numerically reproduces global ILR; do not plot it as another independent baseline. Permuted residuals support an alignment interpretation but do not isolate stumps, blend weights, or the selection rule.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Calibration and degenerate-region sanity comparators\\\",\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"supporting\\\",\n      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Uncalibrated residual quantiles, the full simplex and a singleton center are available for supplementary checks of coverage–mass interpretation. They are not competing learned methods and need not dominate the main narrative.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"results_focus\\\": [\n    \\\"Main comparison: four scientifically meaningful methods, grouped by homoscedastic (H), boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic (B), and shifted heteroscedastic (S) conditions, with dimension 3 versus 10 treated as adaptations under one thesis rather than independent experiments.\\\",\n    \\\"Show coverage at nominal 0.95 and 0.90 separately, not only absolute errors. Show reference mass separately from coverage so that an apparently small region cannot hide undercoverage. Efficiency is probability mass under the fixed mixed probe distribution, not geometric volume, expected cardinality, or a distribution-free quantity.\\\",\n    \\\"Keep fixed-subgroup evidence in the main paper: a compact six-condition table compares the local procedure, global ILR and permuted-residual control using mean absolute subgroup coverage error over both groups and both levels. Supporting tables retain the signed per-group, per-level coverage and per-seed Wilson intervals; empirical group diagnostics are not conditional guarantees.\\\",\n    \\\"Homoscedastic cases show little benefit and small aggregate-utility disadvantages relative to global ILR; do not omit them. Boundary heteroscedastic cases support the strongest joint subgroup-error/reference-mass comparison. Boundary concentration and heteroscedasticity change together, so neither factor is independently causally identified.\\\",\n    \\\"Shifted cases have favorable observed coverage relative to the matched global comparator, but exchangeability is not supplied by the unweighted algorithm. In shifted dimension 3 the local method has larger reference mass than global ILR, so do not claim uniformly smaller regions or a free efficiency gain.\\\",\n    \\\"An aggregate main-text table may report mean utility 0.8772268474776957 with its stored 95% seed-bootstrap interval [0.8766236679621646, 0.8781275287778212], mean marginal absolute coverage error 0.011729600694444453, mean fixed-group absolute coverage error 0.015105333854689552, and reference mass 0.4508402082655165, with sensible rounding. Sources: final.metric.* entries with matching names. Define utility from evaluator code as 1 - [0.375 marginal error + 0.375 group error + 0.25 reference mass]; it is a chosen evaluation weighting, not universal reliability.\\\",\n    \\\"The aggregate local-minus-global utility difference is 0.04762325792650014 (final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap); its stored paired six-seed bootstrap interval is [0.043901240346666696, 0.050616967867983816] (final.evidence.episodes). Interpret it as a fixed-six-case aggregate, not evidence of per-case superiority or proof that stumps improve the original ridge-scale method.\\\",\n    \\\"Final uncertainty uses six seeds [1009, 2003, 3001, 4001, 5003, 6007] and stored 2000-resample paired seed-bootstrap intervals. Repeated copies of the same aggregate interval inside episodes are one estimate, not six independent estimates. Coverage Wilson intervals describe within-seed test hits; do not average interval endpoints or use a mass Wilson interval as an independent-query population interval.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"figure_plan\\\": [\n    \\\"Figure 1, early conceptual overview: interior-simplex response -> ILR center and training-only positive scale -> held-out normalized-score calibration -> separate coverage, subgroup and reference-mass evaluation; mark the shifted condition outside the exchangeability guarantee.\\\",\n    \\\"Figure 2, main reliability comparison: four panels in a 2-by-2 grid for dimension and nominal level, comparing the four main methods across H/B/S with all six seed points and an arithmetic-mean marker. Nominal coverage is a horizontal line, not an attainment-rate label.\\\",\n    \\\"Figure 3, companion efficiency comparison: two dimension panels comparing reference mass across H/B/S and the same four methods, explicitly averaged over the two nominal levels within each seed. Read alongside Figure 2 and the main subgroup table, never as a standalone superiority ranking.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"figure_specs\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"conceptual_overview\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Make the target, training/calibration separation, learned scale, and limited meaning of the evidence legible before quantitative results.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"diagram\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"generate-concept-figure\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"scientific_object_and_information_split\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"not_applicable_conceptual_only\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"training_calibration_and_evaluation_roles\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"The inspected ICLR paper.sty gives one 5.5-inch text column and a 9-inch text height; full width is also column width here. Target a 5.5-by-1.9-inch overview on page 1 or 2, about 0.25 page including caption. One left-to-right schematic with at most six short labeled blocks and a shift caveat is readable at 9-point text; avoid mini-panels, data-shaped decorative regions and invented observations.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 1,\n      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 1,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"coverage_by_condition\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Expose condition-dependent empirical reliability, both nominal levels, and the absence of a uniform advantage.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"faceted_seed_dot_plot\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"H_B_S_condition\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"empirical_joint_composition_coverage\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"dimension_3_or_10_and_nominal_coverage_0.95_or_0.90\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Use the full 5.5-inch single-column width, approximately 4.2 inches high, about 0.55 page including caption. Four panels in two rows leave each plotting area roughly 2.5 inches wide; restrict to three condition labels, four method colors, six small offset seed dots per method and one shared legend. Labels at least 9 points, ticks at least 8 points; use the same coverage axis across panels and avoid connecting H/B/S as a trajectory. Show seed variability rather than inventing condition-specific bootstrap intervals.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 4,\n      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"reference_mass_by_condition\\\",\n      \\\"required\\\": true,\n      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Compare the specific measured size proxy without hiding coverage failures or the shifted dimension-3 mass increase.\\\",\n      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"faceted_seed_dot_plot\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"H_B_S_condition\\\",\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"reference_mass_averaged_over_nominal_levels_within_seed\\\",\n      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"dimension_3_or_10\\\",\n      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Target 5.5 by 2.5 inches across two side-by-side panels, about 0.35 page including caption. Three categories and four shared method series fit without shrinking labels; use seed dots, mean markers, a shared legend and a common mass axis. State the alpha averaging and mixed reference measure explicitly. Do not combine with the four-panel coverage figure into an unreadable six-panel figure.\\\",\n      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"supplementary_plan\\\": {\n    \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Provide reconstructable methods, complete observed uncertainty and diagnostics, and transparency about missing comparisons after references, while leaving every result and limitation essential to the central thesis in the main paper.\\\",\n    \\\"planned_subsections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Complete Conditional Scale-Selection Specification\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Use the inspected selected program and METHOD.md for the quadratic ridge features, tail target 0.9, 15 candidate threshold quantiles, minimum leaf size, learning rate 0.1, maximum 80 stump updates, three deterministic folds, constant and four log blends, fitting-only alignment, simplicity ordering, all-data refit, scale floors and evaluator clipping. Explain that the one-standard-error rule is a heuristic, not a significance test. Include pseudocode derived from source, not selected-component frequencies that were never recorded. Final evidence binds the evaluated method; source linkage is documentary unless independently verified later.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Generator, Splits, Geometry and Reference Measure\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document task/evaluate_final.py and task/resources/final/manifest.json: six-dimensional Gaussian covariates; H/B/S generator equations; dimension 3/10; center-fit, scale-fit, calibration and test sizes 1024/1024/512/2048; the test first-covariate shift 1.25; shared ridge center; exact ILR basis; strictly positive responses; 128 selected test covariates and 1024 probes per query from a half Gaussian-in-ILR/half uniform-simplex mixture. Explain query clustering, data-dependent reference covariance, and why mass is not geometric volume. No exact-zero, finite-count or real-data experiment is implied.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Full Seed-Level Coverage and Efficiency Tables\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Tabulate all final cases and seeds, both alphas, both fixed sign-of-first-covariate groups, all four main methods, and available within-seed Wilson intervals. Retain signed coverage deviations as well as absolute errors and reference mass; do not infer general conditional coverage. Show stored aggregate paired intervals once and describe the fixed-case, seed-resampling unit. Tables can be generated solely from the final.evidence.episodes pointer.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.marginal_coverage_error\\\", \\\"final.metric.group_coverage_error\\\", \\\"final.metric.reference_mass\\\", \\\"final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Alignment, Calibration and Degenerate-Region Checks\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Report constant/global numerical agreement, permuted-training-residual results, uncalibrated residual-quantile baseline, full-simplex and singleton-center coverage/mass, using their stored final records. State what each check tests and cannot establish. Do not invent a stump-only, ridge-only, no-selection, interaction-depth, sample-size, dimension-sweep or weighted-shift ablation.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.constant_control_score\\\", \\\"final.metric.permuted_control_score\\\", \\\"final.metric.control_pass_fraction\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Conditional Rank Argument and Its Boundaries\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Give the standard split-conformal rank argument conditional on the center-fit and scale-fit data: a fixed positive scale preserves exchangeable calibration/test scores; k=ceil((m+1)(1-alpha)) with infinity for k>m gives marginal coverage under the stated assumptions. Cover ties and common clipping. Do not label it a new theorem, a group-coverage guarantee, or shifted-distribution validity; novelty and supporting citations need independent literature verification.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Reproducibility and Evaluation Scope\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document the available NumPy 2.1.3 requirement, evaluator Python 3.12 statement, six final seeds, synthetic-data generation and no external-data download, artifact hashes, available commands and per-seed stored elapsed durations with their limited meaning. Distinguish inspected code, recorded executions and commands verified later. Transparently record that the latent-count bridge was not implemented and the interaction attempt has no usable measured performance; root cause is unknown. Search counts and deltas are omitted because study.* sources are absent. Final qualification is completed/qualified, not proof of generalization. Provide anonymous relative reproduction paths and a table/figure-to-generator mapping when those scripts exist.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\", \\\"final.qualification.correct\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"LLM Usage\\\",\n        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Disclose the evidenced roles of LLM assistance in ideation, candidate-source editing, framing, literature-query planning and subsequent writing, without asserting unrecorded manual verification or completed experiments. Avoid model/version attributions unless logged. Record prohibited-literature encounters only if an actual encounter is documented; none was encountered through network access in this strategy stage.\\\",\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \\\"forbidden_headings\\\": [\n    \\\"Search Progression\\\", \\\"Campaign Phases\\\", \\\"Milestones\\\", \\\"First Valid Result\\\", \\\"Iteration History\\\", \\\"Experiment A\\\", \\\"Experiment B\\\", \\\"Winner Selection\\\", \\\"From Failure to Success\\\", \\\"Latent-Count Bridge Results\\\", \\\"Quantile-Stump Ablation\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"forbidden_phrases\\\": [\n    \\\"we then tried\\\", \\\"subsequent improvements\\\", \\\"first valid result\\\", \\\"next-best\\\", \\\"later variants\\\", \\\"search winner\\\", \\\"correctness rate\\\", \\\"correct and eligible\\\", \\\"eligible\\\", \\\"combined_score\\\", \\\"rubric\\\", \\\"worker_01\\\", \\\"worker_02\\\", \\\"state of the art\\\", \\\"universally reliable\\\", \\\"uniformly smaller regions\\\", \\\"guaranteed coverage under covariate shift\\\", \\\"distribution-free conditional validity\\\", \\\"quantile stumps caused the gain\\\", \\\"the latent bridge restored coverage\\\", \\\"unscored attempts were measured losses\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"notes_for_writer\\\": [\n    \\\"Draft no paper during strategy generation. This plan is for write-paper-standalone. Later writing, review, audit and revision must independently re-read final.qualification.*; retain any failed qualification and its limitations if the authoritative artifacts change. Here completed/qualified/true is explicit; unspecified diagnostics remain unknown, and no statistical-versus-adaptivity cause should be invented.\\\",\n    \\\"Qualification source IDs are final.qualification.status, final.qualification.outcome and final.qualification.correct. Scientific result records are final.evidence.episodes and final.metric.*. The episode grounding entry is a pointer, not an embedded value; follow its json_pointer to the actual episodes. Source-inspected equations and implementation facts additionally require the named local files; do not imply those details were candidate diagnostics verified by the evaluator.\\\",\n    \\\"Use ICLR 2026 per VENUE.md, not a newly chosen conference. Inspected .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex and paper.sty: a single 5.5-inch column, 9-inch text height, normal float width equal to text width. All required figures use preferred_span=full_width meaning that sole text column; no fictitious two-column 0.48-width placement. Keep body between 8 and 9 pages; target 8.5 substantive pages and at least 75% occupancy if ending at 8 pages.\\\",\n    \\\"Body allocation including associated floats: abstract/introduction with early overview 1.25 pages; focused related work 0.65; target, geometry, scale procedure and short rank justification 1.85; experimental design, metrics, controls and uncertainty 1.15; condition-structured results with coverage/mass figures and two concise tables 2.40; discussion and limitations 0.95; conclusion 0.25. Total 8.50 pages. Use extra space for the exact reference-measure definition, subgroup contrasts and mechanism caveats, never chronology, repeated tables or generic filler. Keep the main-text limitations and negative H results even if page pressure requires moving detailed pseudocode after references.\\\",\n    \\\"Suggested main headings: Introduction; Related Work; Compositional Regions and Training-Only Scale Learning; Evaluation Design; Coverage and Efficiency Across Conditions; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Within results use Homoscedastic Controls, Boundary-Concentrated Heteroscedasticity, and Covariate-Shift Stress Test. These are three conditions of one study, not three discovery stages.\\\",\n    \\\"Main Table 1: four-method aggregate utility and decomposed errors/mass, with stored local-minus-comparator paired intervals explicitly aggregate over the fixed six cases. Main Table 2: six-condition local/global/permuted subgroup-error comparison. Do not manufacture cell-specific confidence intervals from the overall paired interval; distinguish descriptive means from stored inferential summaries.\\\",\n    \\\"Required quantitative figures must derive every seed point from final.evidence.episodes, export PDF and PNG from the same source with PNG at 300 DPI, and use the PDF in LaTeX whenever available. All full-width raster-only assets must be regenerated at at least 2400 pixels wide, never upscaled; any later column-width-only raster must be at least 1200 pixels wide. At most one overview uses generate-concept-figure; do not create that image in this strategy stage. Preserve editable concept source and provenance later, and record any necessary generator fallback rather than silently changing ownership.\\\",\n    \\\"The shared reference distribution is a training-dependent mixture, not invariant simplex volume. Probe-hit Wilson intervals are evaluator-provided binomial-style summaries over clustered query/probe hits; they do not quantify all query-sampling uncertainty. Six seeds and a fixed condition grid limit bootstrap precision and external validity. Do not pool all test examples across seeds as if one independently fitted model generated them.\\\",\n    \\\"Do not equate composition-region coverage with class-label prediction-set coverage, calibration of class probabilities, latent multinomial-probability recovery, or true conditional-probability identification. Outputs here are strictly positive continuous logistic-normal responses, not softmax predictions from an evaluated classifier, exact-zero compositions, CIFAR data or finite-count observations.\\\",\n    \\\"No recorded component-selection frequency, matched repeated ridge-scale comparison, interaction ablation, sensitivity study or real-data test exists. Neither control success nor a favorable aggregate utility attributes the effect to quantile stumps. The original ridge scale remains a documented component and an untested matched-final comparator, not a demonstrated inferior method.\\\",\n    \\\"References and provenance exist, but retrieval metadata alone does not establish relevance or that full text was read. Lei2016DistributionFreePI has a directly relevant title match and is in the allowed starter lineage; verify actual consulted content before citation. Do not fill Related Work with unrelated automatically retrieved entries. Verify suitable ILR, quantile and conditional-coverage context through permitted sources before claiming novelty. Do not identify, search for, open or use the prohibited related paper or citing/discussing pages; stop and record any accidental encounter. This strategy made no network requests.\\\",\n    \\\"For double-blind review remove author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding and identifying links; do not copy the template's explicit author or final-copy setting. Preserve any required automatically managed watermark block. Include separate LLM Usage after references and any actually required venue checklist; do not invent a checklist requirement from another conference or claim this local style was independently certified as the latest official release.\\\",\n    \\\"All supplementary source_ids below are registered result anchors; implementation, resource and process statements also name their actual local documentary sources and are not falsely recast as measured findings. For LLM-use details use the available process logs and this strategy's record, not the episode pointer as sole evidence. No definitive posthoc analysis ranking is assigned; route the separately authoritative appendix-ranked analyses to Supplementary Information without revising this plan retroactively.\\\",\n    \\\"Keep qualitative memory private. Do not use global_memory_summary.md, idea names, or old incomplete status prose to override completed final results. Internal names inside this strategy are private-only grounding instructions, never recommended visible manuscript labels. No final-paper validation, paper.tex, or claims_grounding.json is produced in this stage.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n}\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
